title: write commands silently create a half-board, then init is locked out forever
updated: 2026-08-10T01:27:58Z
---

Any write command creates a partial board, and that partial board then locks
`init` out permanently:

    mkdir p && cd p && git init -q . && git commit -q --allow-empty -m init
    karr create "oops wrong dir"    -> rc=0, "Created task 1"
    git for-each-ref refs/karr/     -> meta/next-id + tasks/1/data, but NO refs/karr/config
    karr init --name "My Real Board"-> rc=1 "Board already exists in refs/karr/"
    karr config get board.name      -> "Kanban Board"   # can never be set now

`board_exists` (`BoardStore.pm:38-41`) accepts `meta/next-id` on its own, so the
half-board blocks `init` forever and the user never gets the `.gitignore`
entries `init` writes. The only way out is `karr destroy --yes`.

Every other command (`board`, `context`, `config`, `materialize`, `pick`, `move`,
`disable`) also happily operates on a nonexistent board and returns 0. Typing
`karr create` in the wrong directory is an easy mistake, and it leaves refs
behind in an unrelated repo.

kanban-md refuses: `internal/config/config.go:19` — `"no kanban board found (run
'kanban-md init' to create one)"`.

Fix: require `refs/karr/config` for a board to count as existing; make write
commands refuse with that message when it is absent; make `init` complete a
half-board instead of refusing.
Fixed and merged into main; full suite green at 88 files / 566 tests.

Every one of these was reproduced before it was fixed and left a regression test
that was verified to fail against the pre-fix code -- not a test that merely
passes now. Details and what was deliberately NOT fixed are in the merge commit
that names this ticket.
